WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:01.664
The first rough plot

00:00:01.664 --> 00:00:03.017
of the Mandelbrot Set

00:00:03.017 --> 00:00:04.841
appeared in a 1978 paper

00:00:04.841 --> 00:00:06.819
by the mathematicians Robert Brooks

00:00:06.819 --> 00:00:08.280
and J. Peter Matelski.

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:10.975
Soon after, Benoit Mandelbrot,

00:00:10.975 --> 00:00:12.466
a researcher at IBM

00:00:12.566 --> 00:00:14.680
who had access to more computing power,

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:16.533
also discovered the set.

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:19.600
This led to further explorations.

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:22.100
These early computer graphics were crude,

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:23.747
but the patterns revealed the presence

00:00:23.747 --> 00:00:25.966
of something far more complex.

00:00:26.066 --> 00:00:28.341
Even with fuzzy

00:00:28.341 --> 00:00:30.666
poorly made pictures

00:00:30.733 --> 00:00:31.533
in the eighties

00:00:31.533 --> 00:00:33.229
they were able to really glean

00:00:33.229 --> 00:00:35.076
a lot of interesting insight

00:00:35.076 --> 00:00:36.633
into what's going on.

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:38.830
Mandelbrot went on to popularize his now

00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:40.433
eponymous set to the world

00:00:40.666 --> 00:00:41.664
and became known

00:00:41.664 --> 00:00:43.666
as the Father of Fractals.

00:00:43.733 --> 00:00:45.254
Today, mathematicians

00:00:45.254 --> 00:00:46.512
can use computers to explore

00:00:46.512 --> 00:00:47.655
the Mandelbrot set

00:00:47.655 --> 00:00:49.500
in far greater detail.

00:00:49.733 --> 00:00:51.000
And as soon as you have images

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:52.590
it just opens up

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:53.733
this whole world

00:00:53.733 --> 00:00:55.733
people didn't know about.

